gm steve,

please let me know which ARC version your multia has (3.10 or
later -- 3.5-7 or earlier) and if your power-up sequence stops
at the blue screen and displays an error message....also, if
you are getting any 'beep codes' and if so what the sequence is.

one thing that confuses me is: "create a 20mb partition" (from
ARC).  (iirc) arcinst sets up the paramaters for loading the NT
system and setupldr.exe 'lays down' the software and there is
nothing about setting up specific partitions.

this stuff is from 'memory' and may not be correct:

ntoskrnl.exe is the nt kernel loader

the irql 7 error may be related to (under SRM) pka0 which, under
the multia, is assigned to SCSI Bus ID 7.  when it says "FALSE",
it may not be seeing the SCSI bus and therefore not able to apply
ntoskrnl.exe.

if you know how to bring up the SRM console, do so, and send me
the results (to get out of SRM type ARC at the >>> prompt).  here
is an example of what i would like to see (taken from the SRM
console on one of my machines):

Testing Memory from 8 to 64 meg...
Executing power-up script...
Testing TGA
version                 BL5 V3.8-1 Jun 22 1995 18:10:45

dka0.0.0.6.0           DKA0               TOSHIBA MK2526FB  908B
dka600.6.0.6.0         DKA600      MEDIAVIS RENO CD-ROMX2A  2.04
dva0.0.0.0.1           DVA0                               
ewa0.0.0.8.0           EWA0              08-00-2B-E5-A6-33
pka0.7.0.6.0           PKA0                  SCSI Bus ID 7

64 Meg of system memory

*** keyboard not plugged in...
Keyboard error; using serial port terminal
>>>

in case it would scroll by, you can use the SRM command:
 show device  and it will produce similar results.  if you do not
see PKA0 then the SCSI chip is not being seen....at which point,
power down your machine, remove the external scsi device, power
on the machine and do a SHOW DEVICE again --- if it is still not
there -- let me know, and i will tell you what to check next.

oh, ignore the keyboard error above (specific to my machine).
(fwiw) i have a multia connected to the serial port of a P2 runn-
ing FreeBSD 3.0 and am working on the alpha port of FreeBSD 3.0
under the SRM console.

p.s. i'm sending a copy of this to the list, just in case i told
you something that may not be correct (smile).

good luck,  curtis

