Subject: Alphas and cable modems

OK, so I got the cable modem installed last week, but....I chickened out
(it would REALLY suck to have an Alpha croak during production) and put it
on the "family" Intel machine.  Used WinProxy to share it and the Alphas
like it just fine.

The above was just a response to those curious from my mention of
installation last week.  If you just wanna know how cool a cablemodem is,
keep reading, otherwise consider this the end of the message :)

Cable Modem Review
How is it?  OH MY GOD!  The 32 (36?) meg download of the latest Mediaplayer
is listed on the MS site as 96 minutes or so on a 56K modem....pulled that
puppy down in about two minutes!  I can actually WATCH streaming video now,
webpages are virtually instant, email takes longer to sort than to
download, my Quake2 ping is often below 70 (yes, I'm at last an lpb ;), and
and and....just think... 1.5 megabits...both ways!

To sum it up...if you can get a cablemodem where you are, do it RIGHT NOW!
:)

Alpha friendly?
>From all indications during the install I don't see any reason to expect
there to be a problem hooking it to an Alpha.  There was NO software
installed, just TCP configuration.  Granted, it sounded a bit like a NASA
pre-launch with the three(!) installers here putting it in.  "It" is simply
an intense looking box  about the size of a JAZ drive which is connected to
it's own net card in the Intel.  By the way, if you have a LAN you'll need
two net cards in your machine, one for the modem and one for the LAN.  

I used WinProxy to perform the modem sharing from the Win95 machine and it
went fairly smoothly.  Well, except for the two days of reconfiguring hell
that resulted in the discovery that the only problem was being caused by a
dying net card on the Intel.  Heh.

Be aware that some cable modem setups are oneway only, using a telephone
line at the same time.  I understand that this type requires some .dlls and
whatnot so your Alpha mileage may vary.  My provider is MediaOne (
www.mediaone.com ) if you are able to get them wherever you may be.

Anyway, sorry if this wordy dissertation bothered anybody but I thought a
few of you might be curious.

-David Hopkins
